std::vector<Index> filter_or(const std::vector<Index>& items, const BookIds& ids) {
    std::vector<Index> out;
    for (const auto& x : items)
        if (ids.contains(x.book_id)) out.push_back(x);
    return out;
}

std::vector<Index> filter_and(const std::vector<Index>& items, const BookIds& ids) {
    // same filter if `items` already contains the candidate universe;
    // AND usually means "present in both collections" after you built `ids`
    std::vector<Index> out;
    for (const auto& x : items)
        if (ids.contains(x.book_id)) out.push_back(x);
    return out;
}

std::vector<Index> filter_not(const std::vector<Index>& items, const BookIds& ids) {
    std::vector<Index> out;
    for (const auto& x : items)
        if (!ids.contains(x.book_id)) out.push_back(x);
    return out;
}
```

### If you have two result sets
- **OR**: union `book_id`s into one `unordered_set`
- **AND**: count hits per `book_id` and keep only those with count == all inputs
- **NOT**: keep records from left side whose `book_id` is absent from right side

### Safe choices
- `std::unordered_set` for fast membership checks.
- `std::set` only if you need sorted `book_id`s.
- Preserve original record order by filtering the original `std::vector<Index>`; don’t rebuild from the set.

